That tracks as a learned state-control skill, but I’d separate two mechanisms that can feel similar from the inside.

Locking in is attentional narrowing: suppress irrelevant inputs, reduce self-monitoring, prioritize the task, and keep acting despite discomfort. That overlaps with flow, compartmentalization, and trained performance under stress.

Dissociation is more specifically a reduction in integration between attention, emotion, bodily sensation, memory, or sense of self/reality. In a hard situation, that detachment can temporarily make someone extraordinarily functional—but if the brain learns “disconnect = perform,” it can start deploying the same mechanism when you don’t want it.

The higher-order skill isn’t merely being able to leave the normal state. It’s bidirectional control:

baseline → narrow/compartmentalize → execute → deliberately reintegrate → baseline

Being able to come fully back—body sensation, emotions, surroundings, temporal continuity—is as important as being able to lock in. If you can reliably choose both the entry and the exit, that’s much closer to controlled state regulation than uncontrolled dissociation.